## Mark the depth axis
The depth runs from the low tide of 1.7 m up to the high tide of 5.5 m, so scale the vertical axis from 0 to 6 m in steps of 1 m.
## Find the time between high tides
High tide is at 02:00 and again at 14:34. Measure the horizontal axis in hours after midnight, scaled in steps of 2 hours from 0 to 24.
\text{period} &= \left(14 + \frac{34}{60}\right) - 2
&\approx 12.57 \text{ hours}
\text{half the period} &= \frac{12.57}{2}
&\approx 6.28 \text{ hours}
## Plot the key points
$P$ is the first high tide at $(2, 5.5)$. Low tide is half a period later, at about 08:17 ($t \approx 8.3$), with depth 1.7 m. The next high tide is at 14:34 ($t \approx 14.6$) and the next low tide is at about 20:51 ($t \approx 20.9$).
## Join the points with a smooth cosine curve
The curve is like a cosine wave: it peaks at $P$, falls to the low tide, rises to the second high tide and falls again. It is centred on a depth of $\frac{5.5 + 1.7}{2} = 3.6$ m.
ANSWER: \text{Graph drawn with labelled and scaled axes, with } P(2,\,5.5) \text{ as a high tide}

## Find the period
The period of $\cos ct$ is $\frac{2\pi}{c}$. It is the time between two successive high tides: 02:00 to 14:34.
\text{period} &= 12\frac{34}{60} \text{ hours}
## Solve for c
\frac{2\pi}{c} &= 12\frac{34}{60}
c &= \frac{2\pi}{12\frac{34}{60}}
c &\approx 0.49999
c &\approx 0.5 \text{ (1 decimal place)}
ANSWER: c = 0.5

## Write the probability for each day
Answers: $P(A)=\frac15$. Does not answer: $P(N)=1-\frac15=\frac45$.
Days 1 to 7 in order are $N, A, N, A, N, A, N$: three answers and four misses.
## Multiply the seven probabilities
P &= \frac45\times\frac15\times\frac45\times\frac15\times\frac45\times\frac15\times\frac45
&= \left(\frac45\right)^4\left(\frac15\right)^3
&= \frac{256}{625}\times\frac{1}{125}
&= \frac{256}{78125}
ANSWER: \frac{256}{78125}

## Split the seven days
The 4th answer is on day 7, so days 1 to 6 contain exactly 3 answers, and day 7 is an answer.
## Count the ways to place 3 answers in 6 days
\binom{6}{3} &= \frac{6\times5\times4}{3\times2\times1}
&= 20
## Multiply by the probabilities
P &= \binom{6}{3}\left(\frac15\right)^3\left(\frac45\right)^3\times\frac15
&= 20\times\frac{1}{125}\times\frac{64}{125}\times\frac15
&= \frac{1280}{78125}
&= \frac{256}{15625}
ANSWER: \frac{256}{15625}

## Find each journey time in hours
Time $=$ distance $\div$ speed.
t_{\text{Mary}} &= \frac{260}{96}
t_{\text{Jane}} &= \frac{260}{112}
## Subtract the times
\frac{260}{96}-\frac{260}{112} &= \frac{65}{24}-\frac{65}{28}
&= \frac{65}{168}
&= 0.3869\text{ hours}
## Convert to minutes
0.3869\times60 &= 23.21
&\approx 23\text{ minutes}
ANSWER: 23\text{ minutes}

## Find |AF| using Pythagoras in triangle AEF
|AF|^2 &= 12^2+5^2
&= 169
|AF| &= 13
## Match corresponding sides of the similar triangles
In $\triangle AFE$ and $\triangle DAE$, the right angles match, so $[AD]$ (opposite the right angle in $\triangle DAE$) corresponds to $[AF]$ (opposite the right angle in $\triangle AFE$), and $[AE]$ corresponds to $[FE]$.
\frac{|AD|}{13} &= \frac{12}{5}
## Solve for |AD|
|AD| &= \frac{12 \times 13}{5}
&= \frac{156}{5}
&= 31.2 \text{ cm}
ANSWER: |AD| = 31.2 \text{ cm}

## Find |AG|
|AG| &= |AE|+|EG|
&= 12+27
&= 39 \text{ cm}
## Match corresponding sides of triangles AFE and AGB
Both triangles have a right angle ($\angle AEF$ and $\angle ABG$) and share the angle at $A$. So $[AF]$ matches $[AG]$ and $[AE]$ matches $[AB]$.
\frac{|AG|}{|AF|} &= \frac{|AB|}{|AE|}
\frac{39}{13} &= \frac{|AB|}{12}
## Solve for |AB|
3 &= \frac{|AB|}{12}
|AB| &= 3 \times 12
&= 36 \text{ cm}
ANSWER: |AB| = 36 \text{ cm}

## Find |AJ| in kilometres
214 m is $0.214$ km. Jack is that far above the surface, so $|AJ|$ is the radius plus $0.214$.
|AJ| &= 6371+0.214
&= 6371.214 \text{ km}
## Use Pythagoras in triangle AHJ
The line of sight $[JH]$ is a tangent to the earth, so it is perpendicular to the radius $[AH]$. The right angle is at $H$.
|JH|^2 &= |AJ|^2-|AH|^2
|JH| &= \sqrt{6371.214^2-6371^2}
&\approx 52.22
&\approx 52 \text{ km}
ANSWER: |JH| = 52 \text{ km}

## Find the radius of circle s1
The radius $r$ of $s_1$ is horizontal, so it is the side adjacent to the $53^\circ$ angle in a right-angled triangle with hypotenuse $6371$.
\cos 53^\circ &= \frac{r}{6371}
r &= 6371 \cos 53^\circ
&\approx 3834.16 \text{ km}
## Find the length of the circle
l &= 2\pi r
&= 2\pi(3834.16)
&\approx 24091 \text{ km}
ANSWER: 24091 \text{ km}

## Set up a right-angled triangle in the sphere
The sphere has radius 12, so the centre is $12-6=6$ cm above $A$. The hypotenuse is the radius to $B$, which is 12 cm.
## Use Pythagoras
12^2 &= 6^2 + |AB|^2
144 &= 36 + |AB|^2
|AB|^2 &= 144 - 36
|AB|^2 &= 108
|AB| &= \sqrt{108}
&= \sqrt{36 \times 3}
&= 6\sqrt{3}
ANSWER: |AB| = 6\sqrt{3} \text{ cm}

## Use similar triangles in the cone
The cone has height 12 and radius 12. The water level is 6 cm below the vertex, so the small cone above it has height 6 and radius $|CD|$.
\frac{|CD|}{12} &= \frac{6}{12}
## Solve for the radius
|CD| &= 12 \times \frac{6}{12}
&= 6 \text{ cm}
ANSWER: |CD| = 6 \text{ cm}

## Complete the first branches
The probabilities on the branches from one point add to 1.
P(\text{no } R) &= 1 - \frac{1}{3}
&= \frac{2}{3}
## Complete the traffic branches
P(\text{no } T \mid R) &= 1 - \frac{1}{2}
&= \frac{1}{2}
P(\text{no } T \mid \text{no } R) &= 1 - \frac{1}{4}
&= \frac{3}{4}
## Complete the late branches
When it is raining and heavy traffic, $P(L) = \frac{1}{2}$, so $P(\text{no } L) = \frac{1}{2}$.
In any other situation $P(L) = \frac{1}{5}$, so $P(\text{no } L) = 1 - \frac{1}{5} = \frac{4}{5}$.
When it is not raining and no heavy traffic, $P(L) = \frac{1}{8}$, so $P(\text{no } L) = \frac{7}{8}$.
## Outcome: $R$, $T$, $L$
\frac{1}{3} \times \frac{1}{2} \times \frac{1}{2} &= \frac{1}{12}
## Outcome: $R$, $T$, no $L$
\frac{1}{3} \times \frac{1}{2} \times \frac{1}{2} &= \frac{1}{12}
## Outcome: $R$, no $T$, $L$
\frac{1}{3} \times \frac{1}{2} \times \frac{1}{5} &= \frac{1}{30}
## Outcome: $R$, no $T$, no $L$
\frac{1}{3} \times \frac{1}{2} \times \frac{4}{5} &= \frac{4}{30}
## Outcome: no $R$, $T$, $L$
\frac{2}{3} \times \frac{1}{4} \times \frac{1}{5} &= \frac{2}{60}
## Outcome: no $R$, $T$, no $L$
\frac{2}{3} \times \frac{1}{4} \times \frac{4}{5} &= \frac{8}{60}
## Outcome: no $R$, no $T$, $L$
\frac{2}{3} \times \frac{3}{4} \times \frac{1}{8} &= \frac{6}{96}
## Outcome: no $R$, no $T$, no $L$
\frac{2}{3} \times \frac{3}{4} \times \frac{7}{8} &= \frac{42}{96}
ANSWER: \text{Completed tree diagram (blue entries added)}

## Add the outcomes that end in late
P(L) &= \frac{1}{12} + \frac{1}{30} + \frac{2}{60} + \frac{6}{96}
Use a common denominator of 480.
&= \frac{40}{480} + \frac{16}{480} + \frac{16}{480} + \frac{30}{480}
&= \frac{102}{480}
&= \frac{17}{80}
ANSWER: P(L) = \frac{17}{80}

## Write the conditional probability formula
P(R \mid L) &= \frac{P(R \cap L)}{P(L)}
## Find $P(R \cap L)$
Add the two outcomes with $R$ and $L$.
P(R \cap L) &= \frac{1}{12} + \frac{1}{30}
&= \frac{5}{60} + \frac{2}{60}
&= \frac{7}{60}
## Substitute and simplify
P(R \mid L) &= \frac{\frac{7}{60}}{\frac{17}{80}}
&= \frac{7}{60} \times \frac{80}{17}
&= \frac{560}{1020}
&= \frac{28}{51}
ANSWER: P(R \mid L) = \frac{28}{51}

## Find $|DT|$ with Pythagoras
$D$ is due north of $C$ and $T$ is due east of $C$, so the angle at $C$ in triangle $DCT$ is $90^\circ$.
|DT|^2 &= 15^2 + |CT|^2
&= 225 + |CT|^2
|DT| &= \sqrt{225 + |CT|^2}
## Use tan 30° in triangle $DET$
\tan 30^\circ &= \frac{|TE|}{|DT|}
\frac{1}{\sqrt{3}} &= \frac{|TE|}{|DT|}
|TE| &= \frac{|DT|}{\sqrt{3}}
## Substitute $|DT|$
|TE| &= \frac{\sqrt{225 + |CT|^2}}{\sqrt{3}}
&= \sqrt{\frac{225 + |CT|^2}{3}}
ANSWER: |TE| = \sqrt{\frac{225 + |CT|^2}{3}}

## Equate the two expressions for $|TE|$
\sqrt{3}\,|CT| &= \sqrt{\frac{225 + |CT|^2}{3}}
## Square both sides and clear the fraction
3|CT|^2 &= \frac{225 + |CT|^2}{3}
9|CT|^2 &= 225 + |CT|^2
## Isolate $|CT|$
8|CT|^2 &= 225
|CT|^2 &= \frac{225}{8}
|CT| &= \sqrt{\frac{225}{8}}
&= 5.3033
&= 5.3 \text{ m}
ANSWER: |CT| = 5.3 \text{ m}

## Substitute $|CT|$ into the result of (a)
|TE| &= \sqrt{3}\,|CT|
&= \sqrt{3} \times 5.3033
&= 9.1856
&= 9.2 \text{ m}
ANSWER: |TE| = 9.2 \text{ m}

## Set up the right-angled triangle $FCT$
The bank is the straight line through $C$ and $F$, and $CT$ is perpendicular to it. When the tree falls and its top reaches the bank at $F$, $|FT| = |TE|$. Let $\theta = \angle FTC$.
\cos\theta &= \frac{|CT|}{|FT|}
## Replace $|FT|$ with $|TE|$ and then with $\sqrt{3}\,|CT|$
\cos\theta &= \frac{|CT|}{|TE|}
&= \frac{|CT|}{\sqrt{3}\,|CT|}
&= \frac{1}{\sqrt{3}}
## Find the angle
\theta &= \cos^{-1}\left(\frac{1}{\sqrt{3}}\right)
&= 54.7^\circ
ANSWER: \theta = 54.7^\circ

## Find the angle in which the tree hits the bank
The tree hits the bank if it falls within $\theta$ on either side of $TC$, an angle of $2\theta$. All directions make $360^\circ$.
P &= \frac{2\theta}{360}
&= \frac{2(54.7)}{360}
&= \frac{109.4}{360}
&= 0.3039
## Convert to a percentage
P &= 0.3039 \times 100
&= 30.4\%
ANSWER: 30.4\%
